Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage for the requested bowling‑analysis task  

player  
• player_id : primary key to identify every cricketer  
• player_name : to show the bowler’s name in the final output  

ball_by_ball  
• match_id : join‐key to every delivery in a match  
• over_id / ball_id : allows us to count balls (for strike‑rate) and overs (6 balls = 1 over)  
• bowler : the bowler of the current delivery – core column for every bowling metric  
• innings_no : needed when we join other delivery–level tables that also keep innings_no  

batsman_scored  
• match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no : composite key to join to ball_by_ball  
• runs_scored : runs scored off the bat on that delivery (economy rate counts only these runs)  

wicket_taken  
• match_id / over_id / ball_id / innings_no : composite key to join to ball_by_ball  
• player_out : the dismissed batter (not needed for the calculation but useful for checks)  
• kind_out : type of dismissal; we will exclude rows where kind_out IN ('run out', 'retired hurt', …)  
 (only dismissals attributable to the bowler such as ‘bowled’, ‘caught’, ‘lbw’, ‘stumped’, ‘hit wicket’)  

match  
• season_id / match_date / venue : optional contextual information  
• match_id : join‑key when we need the bowler’s best performance “in a single match”  

Conditions that will be useful  
• When counting wickets:  
 WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'obstruction', 'retired hurt')  
• When counting balls for economy/strike: take every row in ball_by_ball where that bowler bowled.  
• When counting runs conceded for economy: join to batsman_scored and SUM(bs.runs_scored).  
• Overs bowled = (COUNT(*) / 6.0).  
• Economy rate = SUM(runs_scored) / (COUNT(*) / 6.0).  
• Strike rate = COUNT(*) / wickets.  
• Best figures per match = combine “wickets” and “runs_conceded” per match; pick the row with max wickets (and min runs if a tie) and format as `wickets||'-'||runs`.

Below are 10 exploratory SQLite queries (each limited to 20 rows) that move from simple inspection to the complex joins required for the final calculation.

```sql
-- 1. Description: See all different kinds of dismissals recorded
SELECT DISTINCT kind_out
FROM wicket_taken
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: Sample bowler IDs present in the ball_by_ball table
SELECT DISTINCT bowler
FROM ball_by_ball
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: Map a few bowler IDs to their names
SELECT DISTINCT p.player_id, p.player_name
FROM player AS p
JOIN ball_by_ball AS b ON p.player_id = b.bowler
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Count how many legal balls each of the first 20 bowlers delivered
SELECT b.bowler, COUNT(*) AS balls_bowled
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
GROUP BY b.bowler
ORDER BY balls_bowled DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Total runs off the bat conceded by each bowler (first 20)
SELECT b.bowler,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS runs_conceded
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS bs
  ON bs.match_id = b.match_id
 AND bs.over_id  = b.over_id
 AND bs.ball_id  = b.ball_id
 AND bs.innings_no = b.innings_no
GROUP BY b.bowler
ORDER BY runs_conceded DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Wickets credited to each bowler (excluding run‑outs etc.)
SELECT b.bowler,
       COUNT(*) AS wickets
FROM wicket_taken AS wt
JOIN ball_by_ball AS b
  ON wt.match_id   = b.match_id
 AND wt.over_id    = b.over_id
 AND wt.ball_id    = b.ball_id
 AND wt.innings_no = b.innings_no
WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'retired hurt', 'obstructing the field')
GROUP BY b.bowler
ORDER BY wickets DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Economy rate for a sample of bowlers (runs per over)
SELECT b.bowler,
       SUM(bs.runs_scored) * 6.0 / COUNT(*) AS economy_rate
FROM ball_by_ball AS b
JOIN batsman_scored AS bs
  ON bs.match_id   = b.match_id
 AND bs.over_id    = b.over_id
 AND bs.ball_id    = b.ball_id
 AND bs.innings_no = b.innings_no
GROUP BY b.bowler
HAVING COUNT(*) >= 30      -- at least five overs bowled
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Strike rate (balls per wicket) for bowlers with ≥5 wickets
WITH balls AS (
    SELECT bowler, COUNT(*) AS total_balls
    FROM ball_by_ball
    GROUP BY bowler
), wkts AS (
    SELECT b.bowler, COUNT(*) AS total_wkts
    FROM wicket_taken AS wt
    JOIN ball_by_ball AS b
      ON wt.match_id   = b.match_id
     AND wt.over_id    = b.over_id
     AND wt.ball_id    = b.ball_id
     AND wt.innings_no = b.innings_no
    WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'retired hurt', 'obstructing the field')
    GROUP BY b.bowler
)
SELECT wkts.bowler,
       balls.total_balls * 1.0 / wkts.total_wkts AS strike_rate
FROM wkts
JOIN balls ON balls.bowler = wkts.bowler
WHERE wkts.total_wkts >= 5
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Match‑wise wickets for every bowler (first 20 rows)
SELECT b.bowler,
       b.match_id,
       COUNT(*) AS wickets_in_match
FROM wicket_taken AS wt
JOIN ball_by_ball AS b
  ON wt.match_id   = b.match_id
 AND wt.over_id    = b.over_id
 AND wt.ball_id    = b.ball_id
 AND wt.innings_no = b.innings_no
WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'retired hurt', 'obstructing the field')
GROUP BY b.bowler, b.match_id
ORDER BY wickets_in_match DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: Match‑wise “wickets‑runs” figures so we can pick best bowling performance
SELECT perf.bowler,
       perf.match_id,
       perf.wickets || '-' || perf.runs_conceded AS best_figures
FROM (
    SELECT b.bowler,
           b.match_id,
           COUNT(*) AS wickets,
           SUM(bs.runs_scored) AS runs_conceded
    FROM wicket_taken AS wt
    JOIN ball_by_ball AS b
      ON wt.match_id   = b.match_id
     AND wt.over_id    = b.over_id
     AND wt.ball_id    = b.ball_id
     AND wt.innings_no = b.innings_no
    JOIN batsman_scored AS bs
      ON bs.match_id   = b.match_id
     AND bs.over_id    = b.over_id
     AND bs.ball_id    = b.ball_id
     AND bs.innings_no = b.innings_no
    WHERE wt.kind_out NOT IN ('run out', 'retired hurt', 'obstructing the field')
    GROUP BY b.bowler, b.match_id
) AS perf
ORDER BY perf.wickets DESC, perf.runs_conceded ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

These ten queries progressively reveal the necessary columns and joins, culminating in a per‑match “wickets‑runs” summary that can be used (together with economy and strike‑rate sub‑queries) to assemble the final bowling statistics requested.
